                                              *  CSS Selectors & Styling *


Question 1:: What is a CSS selector? Provide examples of element, class, and ID selectors.
ANS ==>A CSS selector is a pattern used to select and style HTML elements. It tells the browser which elements the CSS rules should apply to.

EXAMPLE ==>(1) element selector
               // Targets all elements of a specific type.//
                html.
               <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
                css.
               p {
                    color: green;
                 }

            (2) class selector
               // Targets elements with a specific class attribute. Use a . before the class name.//
                html.
                <div class="note">This is a note.</div>
                <p class="note">This is another note.</p>
                css.
                .note {
                 background-color: #ffffcc;
                 padding: 10px;
                }

            (3) id selector
                //Targets one specific element with a unique id. Use # before the ID name.//
                html.
                <h1 id="header">Welcome!</h1>
                css.
                #header {
                font-size: 32px;
                color: navy;
                }

Question 2  : Explain the concept of CSS specificity. How do conflicts between multiple styles get resolved?            
ANS      ==>  CSS specificity is a system that determines which CSS rule is applied when multiple rules target the samehtml      element. When there’s a conflict, the more specific rule wins.
🔹 How Specificity Works
Each selector has a specificity value made up of 4 parts:

| Selector Type                            | Specificity Value     |
|------------------------------------------|-----------------------|
| Inline styles                            | 1000                  |
| ID selector (`#id`)                      | 0100                  |
| Class, pseudo-class (`.class`, `:hover`) | 0010                  |
| Element/tag (`div`, `p`)                 | 0001                  |

 >  When multiple rules apply to the same element, the browser compares their specificity scores and applies the one with the highest value.

 Question 3: What is the difference between internal, external, and inline CSS? Discuss the
advantages and disadvantages of each approach.
ANS   ==> CSS can be applied to HTML in three ways: internal, external, and inline. Here's how each works, along with their advantages and disadvantages:

  (1) inline css

        CSS is written directly inside an HTML element using the style attribute.

    Advantages: 
    * Quick and easy for small changes.
    * Useful for testing or overriding styles.

     Disadvantages:
    * Not reusable.
    * Makes HTML messy and harder to maintain.
    * Hard to apply styles consistently.

  (2) internal css 
            CSS is written inside a <style> tag within the <head> of the HTML document.
      
      Advantages: 
      * Useful for single-page projects.
      * Keeps CSS separate from HTML elements.

      Disadvantages:
      * Not reusable across multiple pages.
      * Can make the HTML file large and cluttered.

  (3) external css
           CSS is placed in a separate .css file and linked using the <link> tag.
    
      Advantages:
      * Clean, reusable, and maintainable.
      * Can be cached by browsers for better performance.
      * Recommended for large or multi-page websites.

      Disadvantages:
      * Requires an extra HTTP request.
      * No styles visible if the CSS file fails to load.

