                                                 *  CSS Flexbox  *


 Question 1: What is CSS Flexbox, and how is it useful for layout design? Explain the terms
            flex-container and flex-item.  
 ANS    ==>CSS Flexbox is a CSS3 layout model designed to help you create responsive and efficient arrangements of elements within a container. It simplifies complex layouts and makes it easier to align items, distribute space, and adapt to different screen sizes without using floats or positioning.    
        * Why is Flexbox Useful:-
              *  Responsive design: Elements adjust their size and position based on the screen size.

              *  Easy alignment: Vertically and horizontally align items with simple properties.

              *  Dynamic sizing: Items can grow or shrink to fill space.

              *  Order control: You can change the visual order of items without changing the HTML structure.                                          

            Flex-container:-
                   This is the parent element that contains flex items. You make an element a flex-container by setting its display property to flex or inline-flex.

             Flex-item  :-
                   These are the direct child elements of the flex-container. They automatically become flexible and can be controlled using various Flexbox properties like flex-grow, flex-shrink, flex-basis, align-self, etc.

 Question 2: Describe the properties justify-content, align-items, and flex-direction used
             inFlexbox.
 ANS     ==> Here’s a clear explanation of the Flexbox properties: justify-content, align-items, and flex-direction all used to control layout and alignment inside a flex-container.
              1. flex-direction :-
                     What it does:
                               * Defines the main axis along which flex items are placed.

                    * Possible values:

                               * row (default): Items are laid out left to right.

                               * row-reverse: Items are laid out right to left.

                               * column: Items are laid out top to bottom.

                               * column-reverse: Items are laid out bottom to top.
               2. justify-content:-
                         What it does:
                                Aligns items along the main axis (horizontally by default).

                         Possible values:

                               * flex-start (default): Items align to the start of the container.

                               * flex-end: Items align to the end of the container.

                               * center: Items are centered along the main axis.

                               * space-between: Equal space between items.

                               * space-around: Equal space around items.

                               * space-evenly: Equal space between and around items.                

                3. align-items  :-
                         What it does:
                                  Aligns items along the cross axis (vertically by default when flex-direction: row).

                         Possible values:

                                   * stretch (default): Items stretch to fill the container.

                                   * flex-start: Items align to the start of the cross axis.

                                   * flex-end: Items align to the end of the cross axis.

                                   * center: Items are centered along the cross axis.

                                   * baseline: Items align based on their text baseline.               

