                                       *  Typography and Web Fonts *

Question 1: Explain the difference between web-safe fonts and custom web fonts. Why
            might you use a web-safe font over a custom font?

ANS     ==>
           1. Web-Safe Fonts
                    Definition:-
                                Web-safe fonts are pre-installed fonts that are available on most operating systems and browsers by default. They require no extra downloading, so they load quickly and display consistently across platforms.

                    Examples:

                           * Arial
                           * Times New Roman
                           * Verdana
                           * Georgia
                           * Courier New
                           * Tahoma

                    Pros:

                            * Fast loading
                            * High compatibility
                            * No extra setup required

                    Cons:

                             *Limited variety and design flexibility

                     2. Custom Web Fonts
                                     Definition:-
                                                Custom web fonts are external fonts that are not guaranteed to be on the user's system. They are loaded via web services (e.g., Google Fonts, Adobe Fonts) or self-hosted files using @font-face. 

                    Pros:
                         
                         * Greater design flexibility
                         * Helps create a unique brand identity

                    Cons:

                         * Slightly slower page load (fonts need to download)     
                         * May cause a flash of unstyled text (FOUT)
                         * Needs fallback fonts in case loading fails

                  *  Why Use Web-Safe Fonts Over Custom Fonts?  
                     
                               Performance: Web-safe fonts don’t require downloading, so they load faster.

                               Simplicity: No need for external font hosting or CSS setup.

                               Compatibility: They are more reliable across older browsers and systems.

                               Fewer dependencies: Works even without an internet connection to external font providers.

   
Question 2: What is the font-family property in CSS? How do you apply a custom Google
            Font to a webpage?

ANS     ==>
           The font-family property in CSS is used to specify the typeface (font) for text content on a webpage.
           It defines a list of fonts in order of preference — the browser uses the first available one.

           Syntax:
                 selector {
                             font-family: "Font Name", fallback-font, generic-family;
                          }
            
            * How to Apply a Custom Google Font to a Webpage

             Step 1: Link the Font in HTML <head>:-
               Go to Google Fonts, choose a font, and copy the <link> tag.

               Example :
                   
                   <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
            
             Step 2: Use the Font in CSS 
                                     
                                      body {
                                             font-family: 'Roboto', sans-serif;
                                           }

